10.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: In the text that is normally read in English the overcoat / greatcoat that Ochumelov is wearing is brought in as a piece of stage business three times, and those three moments are the moments in which his ‘chameleon’ attitude changes.

In Constance Garnett / the standard Russian 1884 text the coat / шинель is first noted when Ochumelov comes on the scene in his official dress and is then made to do the nervous work for him:

* on arrival he is ‘in uniform’ and the coat is kept neat and buttoned while he is self-assured and ready to punish the owner of the dog;
* when the rumour that the dog may belong to General Zhigalov goes round the coat is fussed with / unbuttoned / brushed down as Ochumelov pulls back and becomes obsequious and fearful;
* on the final reversal, when he is sure the dog is not the General’s, the coat is again put straight and he is once more self-important and aggressive.

Those are the three distinct descriptive uses of the garment that are read as the externalisation of Ochumelov’s fearful, shifting mentality. In the Russian original the word шинель / пальто is actually metonymically picked up four times in the course of the scene, the fourth being the closing image of the official fussing with his coat as he leaves, i.e. the third descriptive shift repeated for emphasis. That is the three-shift / four-mention tradition quoted in the literary criticism.

